WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.590 --> 00:00:02.130
- [Instructor] We're told
that given the height h

00:00:02.130 --> 00:00:04.360
and volume V of a certain cylinder,

00:00:04.360 --> 00:00:06.460
Jill uses the formula r is equal

00:00:06.460 --> 00:00:08.570
to the square root of V over pi h

00:00:08.570 --> 00:00:11.440
to compute its radius to be 20 meters.

00:00:11.440 --> 00:00:14.730
If a second cylinder has the
same volume of the first,

00:00:14.730 --> 00:00:17.810
but is 100 times taller,

00:00:17.810 --> 00:00:20.350
what is its radius?

00:00:20.350 --> 00:00:21.310
Pause this video,

00:00:21.310 --> 00:00:24.440
and see if you can figure
this out on your own.

00:00:24.440 --> 00:00:26.120
All right, now let's do this together.

00:00:26.120 --> 00:00:28.900
So first, I always like to
approach things intuitively.

00:00:28.900 --> 00:00:32.190
So let's say the first cylinder
looks something like this,

00:00:32.190 --> 00:00:33.350
like this.

00:00:33.350 --> 00:00:36.510
And then the second cylinder,
here, it's 100 times taller.

00:00:36.510 --> 00:00:37.820
I would have trouble drawing something

00:00:37.820 --> 00:00:39.340
that's 100 times taller.

00:00:39.340 --> 00:00:41.030
But if it has the same volume,

00:00:41.030 --> 00:00:43.060
it's going to have to be a lot thinner.

00:00:43.060 --> 00:00:46.150
So if you want to maintain the volume,

00:00:46.150 --> 00:00:48.760
as you make the cylinder taller,

00:00:48.760 --> 00:00:51.820
and I'm not going anywhere
close to 100 times as tall here,

00:00:51.820 --> 00:00:54.030
you're going to have
to decrease the radius.

00:00:54.030 --> 00:00:55.780
So we would expect a radius

00:00:55.780 --> 00:00:59.070
to be a good bit less than 20 meters.

00:00:59.070 --> 00:01:00.640
So that's just the first intuition,

00:01:00.640 --> 00:01:02.540
just to make sure that we
somehow don't get some number

00:01:02.540 --> 00:01:04.350
that's larger than 20 meters.

00:01:04.350 --> 00:01:07.070
But how do we figure
out what that could be?

00:01:07.070 --> 00:01:09.140
Well, now we can go back to the formula,

00:01:09.140 --> 00:01:10.860
and we know that Jill calculated

00:01:10.860 --> 00:01:12.550
that 20 meters is the radius.

00:01:12.550 --> 00:01:17.080
So 20 is equal to the
square root of V over pi h.

00:01:17.080 --> 00:01:19.920
And if this formula
looks unfamiliar to you,

00:01:19.920 --> 00:01:24.300
just remember the volume of a cylinder is

00:01:24.300 --> 00:01:28.400
the area of either the top or the bottom,

00:01:28.400 --> 00:01:30.743
so pi r squared times the height.

00:01:31.600 --> 00:01:33.370
And if you were to just solve this for r,

00:01:33.370 --> 00:01:36.070
you would have this exact
formula that Jill uses.

00:01:36.070 --> 00:01:38.610
So this isn't some new formula.

00:01:38.610 --> 00:01:41.270
This is probably something
that you have seen already.

00:01:41.270 --> 00:01:43.880
So we know that 20
meters is equal to this,

00:01:43.880 --> 00:01:45.740
and now we're talking about a situation

00:01:45.740 --> 00:01:49.290
where we're at a height
that is 100 times taller.

00:01:49.290 --> 00:01:52.810
So this other cylinder
is going to have a radius

00:01:52.810 --> 00:01:54.090
of square root of,

00:01:54.090 --> 00:01:55.390
V is going to be the same,

00:01:55.390 --> 00:01:57.010
so let's just write that V there.

00:01:57.010 --> 00:01:58.060
Pi doesn't change.

00:01:58.060 --> 00:01:59.520
It's always going to be pi.

00:01:59.520 --> 00:02:00.850
And now instead of h,

00:02:00.850 --> 00:02:03.010
we have something that
is 100 times taller.

00:02:03.010 --> 00:02:05.710
So we could write that as 100h.

00:02:05.710 --> 00:02:08.360
And then what's another way to write this?

00:02:08.360 --> 00:02:10.930
Well, what I'm going to do
is try to bring out the 100,

00:02:10.930 --> 00:02:13.750
so I still get the square
root of V over pi h.

00:02:13.750 --> 00:02:17.810
So I could rewrite this
as the square root of

00:02:17.810 --> 00:02:19.800
one over 100

00:02:20.890 --> 00:02:24.660
times V over pi h,

00:02:24.660 --> 00:02:28.670
which I could write as the
square root of one over 100,

00:02:28.670 --> 00:02:31.350
and I'm just using
properties of radicals here,

00:02:31.350 --> 00:02:33.720
times the square root

00:02:33.720 --> 00:02:37.980
of V over pi h.

00:02:37.980 --> 00:02:40.420
Now, we know what the square
root of V over pi h is.

00:02:40.420 --> 00:02:43.260
We know that that is 20,
and our units are in meters.

00:02:43.260 --> 00:02:45.030
So this is 20.

00:02:45.030 --> 00:02:47.580
And then what's the square
root of one over 100?

00:02:47.580 --> 00:02:48.630
Well, this is the same thing

00:02:48.630 --> 00:02:51.660
as one over the square root of 100.

00:02:51.660 --> 00:02:54.520
And of course, now it's
going to be times 20,

00:02:54.520 --> 00:02:55.640
times 20.

00:02:55.640 --> 00:02:56.473
Well, the square root of 100,

00:02:56.473 --> 00:02:59.370
I should say the principal
root of 100 is 10.

00:02:59.370 --> 00:03:01.370
So the radius of our new cylinder,

00:03:01.370 --> 00:03:04.180
of the second cylinder is
going to be 1/10 of 20,

00:03:04.180 --> 00:03:06.900
which is equal to two meters.

00:03:06.900 --> 00:03:08.050
And we're done.

00:03:08.050 --> 00:03:12.130
The second cylinder is going
to have a radius of two meters,

00:03:12.130 --> 00:03:14.100
which meets our intuition.

00:03:14.100 --> 00:03:17.350
If we increase our height
by a factor of 100,

00:03:17.350 --> 00:03:20.530
then our radius decreases
by a factor of 10.

00:03:20.530 --> 00:03:21.363
The reason why is

00:03:21.363 --> 00:03:24.010
because you square the
radius right over here.

00:03:24.010 --> 00:03:27.070
So if height increases by a factor of 100,

00:03:27.070 --> 00:03:30.150
if radius just decreases
by a factor of 10,

00:03:30.150 --> 00:03:34.143
it'll make this whole expression
still have the same volume.

